WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.266 --> 00:00:01.468 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Hello, Cynthia.
How are you?

00:00:01.468 --> 00:00:02.335 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I'm good, Seth.
How are you?

00:00:02.335 --> 00:00:04.170 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I'm wonderful.
It's always good to see you.

00:00:04.170 --> 00:00:06.039 align:start size:94% position:6%
I want to ask, you know,
I'm coming up

00:00:06.039 --> 00:00:08.108 align:start size:94% position:6%
on about 20 years
of being a New Yorker.

00:00:08.108 --> 00:00:09.275 align:start size:94% position:6%
You've lived here
your whole life.

00:00:09.275 --> 00:00:11.378 align:start size:94% position:6%
Has this time --
Has this difficult time

00:00:11.378 --> 00:00:16.816
reminded you of other eras
of upheaval in the city?

00:00:16.816 --> 00:00:18.084 size:88% position:44% line:92%
-I mean, New York, yeah.

00:00:18.084 --> 00:00:21.421 align:start size:94% position:6%
I've been living here
since I was born in 1966.

00:00:21.421 --> 00:00:25.258
Our city goes through a lo
of crises from time to time.

00:00:25.258 --> 00:00:28.862 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I have to say, yes,
it's been really reminding m

00:00:28.862 --> 00:00:33.333 align:start size:94% position:6%
of the early days of
the AIDS crisis, you know?

00:00:33.333 --> 00:00:37.904 align:start size:94% position:6%
I feel like the same way
Black Lives Matter protester

00:00:37.904 --> 00:00:39.105 line:92%
have taken to the streets

00:00:39.105 --> 00:00:42.042 align:start size:94% position:6%
and are completely
changing our world,

00:00:42.042 --> 00:00:45.612 align:start size:94% position:6%
I feel like that is what
AIDS activists did,

00:00:45.612 --> 00:00:50.050 align:start size:94% position:6%
what LGBT people did,
founding ACT UP,

00:00:50.050 --> 00:00:52.886 align:start size:94% position:6%
taking to the streets,
disrupting traffic.

00:00:52.886 --> 00:00:55.121 align:start size:94% position:6%
And because, similarly,
you know,

00:00:55.121 --> 00:00:57.157 align:start size:94% position:6%
there was an epidemic going on
in their community

00:00:57.157 --> 00:01:01.327
that the rest of the world
seemed basically unaware of,

00:01:01.327 --> 00:01:04.330 size:78% position:39%
and in order to seize
the world's attention

00:01:04.330 --> 00:01:07.367 line:92%
and demand an enormous change,

00:01:07.367 --> 00:01:11.104 align:start size:94% position:6%
that's what LGBT people
did then.

00:01:11.104 --> 00:01:12.338 line:92%
And that's what, you know,

00:01:12.338 --> 00:01:15.308
so many Black Lives Matter
protesters are doing now.

00:01:15.308 --> 00:01:17.477 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I have to say,
when Trump was elected,

00:01:17.477 --> 00:01:19.412 align:start size:94% position:6%
I was so inspired
by the women's marches

00:01:19.412 --> 00:01:21.815 align:start size:94% position:6%
not only in this country,
but across the world.

00:01:21.815 --> 00:01:24.017 line:92%
But I think now the estimate i

00:01:24.017 --> 00:01:28.988 align:start size:94% position:6%
something like 20 million Blac
Lives Matter people have -

00:01:28.988 --> 00:01:32.158 align:start size:94% position:6%
protesters have, across
the country, participated.

00:01:32.158 --> 00:01:36.362
And we've never seen a movemen
like that in this country --

00:01:36.362 --> 00:01:39.299 align:start size:94% position:6%
every race of person,
every ethnicity,

00:01:39.299 --> 00:01:40.200 size:88% position:44% line:92%
every age, every gender.

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:43.002
-You know, I think when yo
talk about these movements

00:01:43.002 --> 00:01:44.170 line:92%
that demand enormous change,

00:01:44.170 --> 00:01:47.307
one of the most important things
is just to educate, right?

00:01:47.307 --> 00:01:48.575 line:92%
Like, people aren't fully aware.

00:01:48.575 --> 00:01:50.443 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I think that, you know
what you've seen

00:01:50.443 --> 00:01:54.814 align:start size:94% position:6%
with just the last five year
of how Black Lives Matter

00:01:54.814 --> 00:01:56.716 line:92%
is perceived as a movement

00:01:56.716 --> 00:01:59.185 align:start size:94% position:6%
a lot more people
obviously think of it

00:01:59.185 --> 00:02:02.856 size:88% position:44%
in a more positive way
as a necessary movement.

00:02:02.856 --> 00:02:06.192 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I think that's also true
in politics.

00:02:06.192 --> 00:02:07.427 size:88% position:44% line:92%
Last time you were here,

00:02:07.427 --> 00:02:10.463 align:start size:94% position:6%
you were running
on a progressive agenda,

00:02:10.463 --> 00:02:12.532 size:84% position:42% line:92%
you know, for governor.

00:02:12.532 --> 00:02:14.167 line:92%
And while you didn't win,

00:02:14.167 --> 00:02:17.370 size:88% position:44%
it does seem that a lot
of what was maybe framed

00:02:17.370 --> 00:02:18.538 line:92%
as a little bit more left,

00:02:18.538 --> 00:02:20.840 align:start size:94% position:6%
a little more, you know,
out of the mainstream

00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:24.444 line:92%
has now been pulled into favor

00:02:24.444 --> 00:02:27.447
Do you feel happy about the fact
that, you know,

00:02:27.447 --> 00:02:31.284
while you did not, you know,
obviously win the election

00:02:31.284 --> 00:02:32.619 align:start size:94% position:6%
it does feel like
a lot of your ideas

00:02:32.619 --> 00:02:36.022 align:start size:94% position:6%
are now viewed
a lot more favorably?

00:02:36.022 --> 00:02:37.223 size:81% position:41% line:92%
-Yes. I mean, I was --

00:02:37.223 --> 00:02:42.962
2018, there was an incredibl
blue wave in New York Stat

00:02:42.962 --> 00:02:44.330 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
that is continuing.

00:02:44.330 --> 00:02:48.368
And we took the New York State
Senate back for the Democrats.

00:02:48.368 --> 00:02:50.069 line:92%
And we've been able to --

00:02:50.069 --> 00:02:51.504 line:92%
in just a couple of years,

00:02:51.504 --> 00:02:55.942
we've been able to pass th
Green New Deal for New York.

00:02:55.942 --> 00:02:58.611 line:92%
We've done major bail reform

00:02:58.611 --> 00:03:03.116 align:start size:94% position:6%
We've had driver's license
extended to everyone,

00:03:03.116 --> 00:03:04.918 line:92%
including undocumented people.

00:03:04.918 --> 00:03:06.186 line:92%
We've updated our abortion laws.

00:03:06.186 --> 00:03:10.023 align:start size:94% position:6%
We've passed the
Gender Expression
Nondiscrimination Act.

00:03:10.023 --> 00:03:11.491 line:92%
It just goes on and on and on.

00:03:11.491 --> 00:03:13.193 line:92%
And one of the exciting things

00:03:13.193 --> 00:03:18.398
is that we're actually two votes
away from a veto-proof majorit

00:03:18.398 --> 00:03:19.866 line:92%
in the New York State Senate

00:03:19.866 --> 00:03:23.670
And so when that happens,
God willing, very soon,

00:03:23.670 --> 00:03:27.373 align:start size:94% position:6%
we will no longer have to
be dependent on the governor

00:03:27.373 --> 00:03:29.275 align:start size:94% position:6%
for passing
all the progressive things

00:03:29.275 --> 00:03:31.878 align:start size:94% position:6%
that we want to have
in New York State and can have

00:03:31.878 --> 00:03:36.249 align:start size:94% position:6%
-You have been critical of bot
the governor and the mayor

00:03:36.249 --> 00:03:40.553
in their handling, particularl
the early days of the pandemic

00:03:40.553 --> 00:03:44.657 align:start size:94% position:6%
But you wrote an op-ed
that you had a unique angle on

00:03:44.657 --> 00:03:48.661 align:start size:94% position:6%
because you are both in sort o
the TV film production

00:03:48.661 --> 00:03:49.963 line:92%
that is so vital to the city

00:03:49.963 --> 00:03:53.333 align:start size:94% position:6%
as well as being a parent
in this city.

00:03:53.333 --> 00:03:54.901 line:92%
This is true of me, as well.

00:03:54.901 --> 00:03:57.437 align:start size:94% position:6%
Can you speak about sort o
what you saw

00:03:57.437 --> 00:03:58.871 align:start size:94% position:6%
and what led you to write
this op-ed

00:03:58.871 --> 00:04:02.909 align:start size:94% position:6%
about how we're valuing one
little bit more than the other

00:04:02.909 --> 00:04:07.880 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yes. I mean, it was kind of
a staggering thing.

00:04:07.880 --> 00:04:10.350 size:84% position:42%
I had both of these two
phone calls in one day.

00:04:10.350 --> 00:04:13.152 align:start size:94% position:6%
And the first
was with the production

00:04:13.152 --> 00:04:15.588
that I'm going to start shooting
at the end of the month

00:04:15.588 --> 00:04:17.490 size:84% position:42%
and all the precautions
that were being taken.

00:04:17.490 --> 00:04:19.959 align:start size:94% position:6%
And the second
was with my son's school

00:04:19.959 --> 00:04:23.029 align:start size:94% position:6%
to hear about what precautions
were being taken there.

00:04:23.029 --> 00:04:29.168 align:start size:94% position:6%
And just the contrast
could've not been more stark

00:04:29.168 --> 00:04:30.169 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
In our production,

00:04:30.169 --> 00:04:33.406
they had an industrial hygienist
walk through all the sets.

00:04:33.406 --> 00:04:36.776
And they were reconfigured
and there were air purifiers

00:04:36.776 --> 00:04:38.411 align:start size:94% position:6%
And the ventilation system
was redone.

00:04:38.411 --> 00:04:43.283
An entire department was created
for COVID safety.

00:04:43.283 --> 00:04:49.289 align:start size:94% position:6%
In my son's school,
which is not particular to him

00:04:49.289 --> 00:04:52.225 align:start size:94% position:6%
but to all the schools
across the city,

00:04:52.225 --> 00:04:56.362 align:start size:94% position:6%
I heard about how airflow
was being gauged

00:04:56.362 --> 00:04:58.398 line:92%
by a piece of toilet paper

00:04:58.398 --> 00:05:02.769 align:start size:94% position:6%
attached with a paper clip
to a yardstick.

00:05:02.769 --> 00:05:05.371 line:92%
You know, in our production,

00:05:05.371 --> 00:05:07.373 align:start size:94% position:6%
you get tested
before you start work.

00:05:07.373 --> 00:05:10.610 align:start size:94% position:6%
Our crew is tested
one to three times a week.

00:05:10.610 --> 00:05:12.378 line:92%
Actors are tested every day.

00:05:12.378 --> 00:05:14.847 align:start size:94% position:6%
People entering
my son's school building,

00:05:14.847 --> 00:05:18.818
whether they choose to be tested
before they started work

00:05:18.818 --> 00:05:20.019 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
was optional.

00:05:20.019 --> 00:05:21.454 align:start size:94% position:6%
And then people
entering the building

00:05:21.454 --> 00:05:24.390 align:start size:94% position:6%
once school officially begin
with kids,

00:05:24.390 --> 00:05:26.426 align:start size:94% position:6%
even temperature taking
is optional.

00:05:26.426 --> 00:05:30.596 align:start size:94% position:6%
So the result is that even
people who chose to be tested,

00:05:30.596 --> 00:05:32.965 align:start size:94% position:6%
by the time they got
the results back,

00:05:32.965 --> 00:05:34.400 line:92%
they had already started work,

00:05:34.400 --> 00:05:37.170 align:start size:94% position:6%
and we have 55 schools
and counting

00:05:37.170 --> 00:05:41.107 align:start size:94% position:6%
in which at least one person
is infected,

00:05:41.107 --> 00:05:42.442 line:92%
and they have now been exposed

00:05:42.442 --> 00:05:44.744 align:start size:94% position:6%
to many other people
in that building.

00:05:44.744 --> 00:05:47.780 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean, I could talk about thi
for a very long time.

00:05:47.780 --> 00:05:49.882 align:start size:94% position:6%
But I just keep
having this thought,

00:05:49.882 --> 00:05:51.584 align:start size:94% position:6%
which is that
starting on Monday,

00:05:51.584 --> 00:05:56.089 align:start size:94% position:6%
the only restaurant you ca
eat at indoors with a mask off

00:05:56.089 --> 00:05:59.459 align:start size:94% position:6%
is going to be a New York City
public classroom.

00:05:59.459 --> 00:06:03.930 align:start size:94% position:6%
-It was a really nice piec
to have written

00:06:03.930 --> 00:06:06.232 align:start size:94% position:6%
insofar that it brought
so much attention to that,

00:06:06.232 --> 00:06:08.434 align:start size:94% position:6%
because it is shocking
when you realize

00:06:08.434 --> 00:06:11.371 align:start size:94% position:6%
how we are able to do it
when we want to,

00:06:11.371 --> 00:06:14.240 size:81% position:41%
and then we just make
very selective choices

00:06:14.240 --> 00:06:16.042 size:75% position:38% line:92%
of where we want to.

00:06:16.042 --> 00:06:18.010 align:start size:94% position:6%
I also want to ask
about "Ratched."

00:06:18.010 --> 00:06:22.815
This is the first -- I guess
the first show you signed up for

00:06:22.815 --> 00:06:23.716 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
after the election.

00:06:23.716 --> 00:06:29.122 align:start size:94% position:6%
This is a prequel to "One Flew
Over the Cuckoo's Nest,"

00:06:29.122 --> 00:06:31.090 align:start size:94% position:6%
the famous Nurse Ratched
from that.

00:06:31.090 --> 00:06:34.460
But, yeah, I would argue you d
not need to have seen the film

00:06:34.460 --> 00:06:36.796 align:start size:94% position:6%
or read the book to be abl
to enjoy this

00:06:36.796 --> 00:06:38.798 size:84% position:42% line:92%
as a freestanding show.

00:06:38.798 --> 00:06:39.699 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-Absolutely not.

00:06:39.699 --> 00:06:41.434 align:start size:94% position:6%
And it's a Ryan Murphy
production,

00:06:41.434 --> 00:06:46.139 align:start size:94% position:6%
which means that the actor
and the actresses in particula

00:06:46.139 --> 00:06:47.807 line:92%
are amazing and very varied.

00:06:47.807 --> 00:06:51.477 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's Sharon Stone
and Sarah Paulson and me

00:06:51.477 --> 00:06:53.913 align:start size:94% position:6%
and Sophie Okonedo
and Amanda Plummer.

00:06:53.913 --> 00:06:56.549 align:start size:94% position:6%
And the list just goes on
and on and on.

00:06:56.549 --> 00:06:59.752 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I get to play
Sarah Paulson's love interest.

00:06:59.752 --> 00:07:06.492
And it is not very often tha
you get even one queer woman

00:07:06.492 --> 00:07:07.260 line:92%
playing a queer character.

00:07:07.260 --> 00:07:10.763
But to have two queer wome
playing two queer characters

00:07:10.763 --> 00:07:14.000 align:start size:94% position:6%
who are romantically involve
with each other

00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:15.635 line:92%
just almost never happens.

00:07:15.635 --> 00:07:18.805
-Well, it's one of the man
great things about the show.

00:07:18.805 --> 00:07:20.640 align:start size:94% position:6%
And it truly is
an incredible cast.

00:07:20.640 --> 00:07:23.109
And it's always great to see you
on-screen again.

00:07:23.109 --> 00:07:25.511 align:start size:94% position:6%
And thank you so much
for being here, Cynthia.

00:07:25.511 --> 00:07:26.612 size:84% position:42% line:92%
It's always a pleasure.

00:07:26.612 --> 00:07:28.981 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Thank you.
Pleasure for me, too.

